  "patterns": [
    {
      "id": "hungry-want-food",
      "pattern": "أَنَا + [Adjective] + وَ + أُرِيدُ + [Noun]",
      "exampleArabic": "أنا جائع وأريد طعاما",
      "exampleTransliteration": "anā jāʾiʿun wa-urīdu ṭaʿāman",
      "exampleEnglish": "I am hungry and I want food.",
      "grammarNote": "Nothing new grammatically here — جَائِع ('hungry') is a plain equational sentence like سَعِيد/حَزِين (levels 7 and 49): أَنَا + adjective, no verb 'to be' needed. أُرِيدُ ('I want', level 15) still takes its object directly, no preposition. This level is otherwise vocabulary-only: مَطْعَم ('restaurant'), لَحْم ('meat'), سَمَك ('fish'), and أُرُزّ ('rice') to build your own food sentences with.",
      "audio": { "id": "phrase_ana_jaaia_wa_ureedu_taaman", "text": "أنا جائع وأريد طعاما" },
      "introducedInLevel": 58
    },
    {
      "id": "eat-meal",
      "pattern": "آكُلُ + [Meal] + فِي + [Place]",
      "exampleArabic": "آكل الغداء في المطعم",
      "exampleTransliteration": "ākulu l-ghadāʾa fī l-maṭʿam",
      "exampleEnglish": "I eat lunch at the restaurant.",
      "grammarNote": "The three meals — فَطُور (breakfast), غَدَاء (lunch) and عَشَاء (dinner) — are ordinary nouns, so they need no new grammar: drop one straight into the eating verb from level 25. آكُلُ + [meal] is the whole pattern. Arabic takes the definite article الـ where English uses none, so it is الغداء (literally \"the lunch\") for the meal you actually ate today. فِي (level 5) then says where. Swap in الفطور or العشاء for the other two meals, and دجاجًا or خضارًا for what was on the plate.",
      "audio": {
        "id": "phrase_aakulu_alghadaa_fi_almatam",
        "text": "آكل الغداء في المطعم"
      },
      "introducedInLevel": 58
    }
  ],
